        "summary": "The Strait of Hormuz is operating under sustained disruption pressure, with the Crisis Pressure composite holding in the extreme band, its top contributor being physical transit deviation. The IMF PortWatch recorded 1 transit on 2026-08-09, the most recent published day in its weekly Tuesday release, against a pre-crisis baseline of 73 per day; that figure is now six days old and should not be read as current throughput. Separately, port arrivals across Hormuz-area ports on both the Persian Gulf and Gulf of Oman sides reached 593 in the most recent 24 hours, reflecting a different methodology and geographic scope that cannot be equated with the PortWatch count. At 11:53 UTC, 421 vessels were holding position away from berth, and 16 indexed events were recorded in the prior 24-hour window. Brent stood at $88.52, up 1.81% over 24 hours, as of 11:53 UTC; futures markets are closed, so that figure is a carried-forward pre-close delta, not a live reading. On the incident side, a single unverified report describes a vessel attacked in the strait; no independent source has confirmed it. Per gCaptain, two ADNOC-affiliated tankers were struck by drones outbound through the strait, with Saudi Arabia and Kuwait condemning the attacks. Iranian state media, not independently confirmed, reports that a separate vessel was struck by an unknown projectile. Iranian officials, per IRNA, continue to assert sovereign authority over the waterway. The Escalation Forecast sits in the elevated band and is rising, diverging from the flat state reading, with Polymarket escalation contracts its top contributor.",
